# Site versioning

The footer shows a version badge, generated from `package.json` by
`src/lib/techStack.js`. It renders **the major only** — `v5`, never `v5.1.1`.

## Why not semantic versioning

Semantic versioning is a promise to people who depend on your code: the major
says "this will break you", the minor says "there is something new", the patch
says "nothing you can see changed". Every one of those is addressed to a
consumer.

**This site has no consumers.** No API, no package, nothing importing it. So
the minor and patch communicate to nobody, and deciding between them is a
judgement call with no correct answer — which in practice means arguing about
whether a background animation is a feature or a style fix, and getting a
different answer each time.

What the major *does* carry here is real and worth keeping: **which generation
of the site this is**. v3 was the Create React App build; v5 is the Next.js
rebuild. "Version 5 of my personal site" is a true and interesting statement.

The other thing a version was doing — signalling the site is alive — is now
done properly by the **last-commit stamp** beside the badge, which links a real
SHA and its timestamp. That is strictly better evidence of recency than a
number someone has to remember to increment.

## When to bump

**Bump the major when a visitor would say the site was rebuilt.** A new
platform, a new design language, a rewrite. That is the only bump.

Everything else — new sections, features, fixes, copy, dependencies — changes
nothing about which generation this is, and the commit stamp already shows the
site is being worked on. Do not bump for them.

```bash
# Only when the site is genuinely a new generation.
npm version 6.0.0 --no-git-tag-version
```

`package.json` keeps a full semver triple because npm requires one; the minor
and patch are simply held at zero and ignored. Never hand-edit the footer
badge — it is derived.

## History

The site previously followed full semantic versioning, with minor bumps for
new sections and patches for fixes. It was retired once the last-commit stamp
landed: the stamp made the patch bumps redundant, and the minor/patch call was
producing more debate than information. Agreed in
[#100](https://github.com/twknab/personal_site_v3/pull/100).

